# modules/cloud/aws/live/checks-dynamodb.rules - the §8.1 DynamoDB read-only
# script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md
# CLOUD-16).  Owned by modules/cloud/aws/live/dynamodb.sh; ids are all
# `CLOUD-DYNAMODB-*`.
#
# A SEPARATE FILE FROM `checks.rules`, for the identical reason
# checks-rds.rules gives in its own header: step 6's tier-2 service PRs are
# genuinely parallel, simultaneously in-flight peers rather than the
# sequential bundles `checks.rules`'s own header describes, so this ticket
# takes the `checks-<service>.rules` escape hatch that header names rather
# than appending to a file every sibling PR would also be appending to.
#
# `coverage-scope: account-region`, and EVERY RECORD BELOW LANDS IN THE
# `<account_id>/<region>` CELL - `dynamodb` is `regional` in
# `_CLOUD_SERVICES` (DynamoDB has no S3-shaped global namespace call; every
# table and every VPC endpoint lives in exactly one region), so the cell the
# pass visits and the region a finding cites are the same value. See
# modules/cloud/aws/live/dynamodb.sh's own header.
#
# NO RECORD BELOW CARRIES A `cis:` VALUE, and that absence is deliberate
# rather than an oversight (docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §1; the same honest-absence
# decision checks-rds.rules documents for two of its own four checks). CIS AWS
# Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 has no DynamoDB section at all - data/cis-
# mappings's full transcribed table (see that file's own header) carries
# sections 1, 2.1 (S3), 2.2 (EBS), 2.3 (RDS), 2.4 (EFS), 3 (logging/
# monitoring) and 5 (networking), and DynamoDB is not among them. Inventing a
# control number here to satisfy a "must cite CIS" habit is the overstated-
# coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15 forbids; an unmapped finding still
# carries its CWE and OWASP category.
#
# CLOUD-DYNAMODB-PUBLIC_ENDPOINT_POLICY-01 IS `confidence: medium`, NOT
# `high`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.  Every other record in this file (and in
# checks-rds.rules, and in checks.rules) reads a TYPED field straight off an
# API response - a boolean, an enum string, a documented sentinel value like
# a snapshot's `restore` attribute naming `all`. This one instead pattern-
# matches the TEXT of a VPC endpoint's policy document for AWS's own default
# "Full Access" shape, because no evaluator API exists for a VPC endpoint
# policy the way `get-bucket-policy-status` exists for S3 - see
# modules/cloud/aws/live/dynamodb_engine.sh's own header for the full
# reasoning. `confidence: medium` states that ceiling honestly rather than
# claiming the same certainty a typed-field read earns.
#
# The type tag is `config-read` on every record, plus `quick`
# (docs/DESIGN.md §5's "passive + config-read, no active probes") -
# `compliance` is omitted from all three, since none carries a `cis` value.

id: CLOUD-DYNAMODB-PUBLIC_ENDPOINT_POLICY-01
title: DynamoDB VPC Gateway endpoint policy is left at the AWS default Full Access shape
script: aws/live/dynamodb.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Attach a scoped endpoint policy to this VPC Gateway endpoint
  naming the specific principals (IAM roles/users), actions and table ARNs it
  is meant to serve, rather than leaving AWS's own generated default in
  place. The default policy grants Principal "*", Action "*", Resource "*"
  with no Condition narrowing it, so any principal that can reach the
  endpoint from inside the VPC can use it to call any DynamoDB action against
  any table in this account and region - it is not an internet exposure, but
  it is a wider set of callers than an operator who deliberately attached an
  endpoint policy likely intended. A policy that legitimately keeps a
  wildcard Principal or Action narrowed by a real Condition (aws:SourceVpc,
  aws:PrincipalOrgID) is not this finding.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-access.html

id: CLOUD-DYNAMODB-NO_ENCRYPTION-01
title: DynamoDB table has no explicit encryption-at-rest configuration
script: aws/live/dynamodb.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Every DynamoDB table is encrypted at rest by default with an
  AWS-owned key whether or not this is configured, so this finding is not
  about missing encryption - it is about the absence of a SEPARATE,
  AUDITABLE key. Enable SSE with an AWS-managed key
  (update-table --sse-specification Enabled=true,SSEType=KMS - no key to
  create or manage, just alias/aws/dynamodb) or a customer-managed key where
  a defined rotation schedule or key-policy separation of duties is
  required. Either gives the table a key policy an operator controls and a
  CloudTrail event each time the key is used, neither of which the default
  AWS-owned key produces.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/EncryptionAtRest.html

id: CLOUD-DYNAMODB-NO_BACKUPS-01
title: DynamoDB table has point-in-time recovery disabled
script: aws/live/dynamodb.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable point-in-time recovery
  (update-continuous-backups --point-in-time-recovery-specification
  PointInTimeRecoveryEnabled=true). Without it, this table can only be
  restored from a manual on-demand backup, if one happens to exist, rather
  than to any second within the last 35 days - so an accidental overwrite, a
  bad application deploy, or a compromised credential that damages this
  table's data is recoverable only as far back as the last manual backup.
  PITR costs storage proportional to the table's own change rate and needs no
  downtime to turn on.
references: CWE-693
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/PointInTimeRecovery.html
